
  Instructions on Printing under Unix

Unix has a number of commands and options for printing. You can either
send a file directly to a port with a printer attached, or you can use the
Unix spooler, called "lpr".

Before explaining the differences between printing directly and via spooler,
we must have a discussion about "setting up" the printer port.

SETTING UP THE PRINTER PORT

         When you print a report you are actually sending the
	 characters that make up the report to one of the
	 computer's "ports." These ports can be used to connect
	 terminals, printers, modems, or other devices. These devices
	 can function at a wide range of speeds, ranging from 100 baud
         for slow "teletypes" to 9600 baud for fast terminals and
	 printers. Accordingly, we cannot configure a port to handle
	 your specific printer or device. 

         This means that prior to using your printer may have to  inform
	 the Unix system about its speed. We have configured both
	 serial ports A and B to a speed of 9600 baud. In addition,
	 we have configured port B as the "login" port. (In Unix-ese,
	 port B is /dev/tty1 and port A is /dev/tty0.) You should
	 only print to a non-login port. Making ports login or non-login is
	 explained fully elsewhere in the Unix documentation.

         We have set up the system for printing to occur on Serial port A,
	 at a speed of 1200 baud, and we have made a "command script",
	 /usr/bin/setprt, to perform the setup details. You can modify this
	 script as needed for your own situation. 

	We have another script "/usr/bin/setprt.image" which is
	intended for use with the Apple Imagewriter. It sends
	at 9600 baud. Please read /usr/bin/setprt.image to leanr
	what you must do to the Imagewriter.

	 It is your
	 responsibility when printing to invoke setprt. You can do this in
	 2 ways: You can issue the setprt command once each session from
	 the keyboard, or you can put the setprt "call" into the /etc/rc
	 file, which is automatically run when you "boot" Unix into
	 multi-user mode with the "init 2" command. If you want to modify
	 /etc/rc, we suggest that you add the 2 indicated lines near the
	 bottom as shown here:


	       rm -f /usr/spool/lpd/lock
	      #	rm -f /usr/spool/vpd/lock
              #	rm  -f /usr/spool/dpd/lock
	      #	rm -f /usr/spool/dpd/glock
	        	/usr/lib/lpd
	      #	/usr/lib/dpd
	      # define terminal parameters
	      /etc/params
====>	      # set up printer
====>	      /usr/bin/setprt
	      fi

PRINTING DIRECTLY AND VIA SPOOLER

         Unix allows the user to print directly or through use of the
	 "spooler". 

         SPOOLER

         The spooler is a set of programs which stack up print
	 jobs and send them to the printer as soon as it has completed its
 	 previous jobs.  In addition, when spooling, you are given control
	 over your terminal even while the report is being printed.
         Assuming that you have a file called "myfile", you
	 print it with the spooler by simply issuing:

		cat myfile | lpr

         The spooler also places a "burst page" between reports so that
	 they are easy to identify.

         There is a problem which sometimes crops up with the spooler,
	 though. Sometimes it gets "stuck" and it needs to be cleaned out.
	 Files awaiting printing are placed into the /usr/spool/lpd
	 directory. They lose the name you gave them, though. Unix assigns
	 them an arbitrary numeric name in that directory. Unix also puts
	 a "lock" file into the directory when the printer is in use. If
	 files are not being printed even though the printer is available,
	 it usually means that there is a bogus lock file. Simply erase it,
	 as seen here:

		cd /usr/spool/lpd
		rm lock

         You also can erase the data files there if you need to.

         DIRECTLY

         You can directly print a file by simply sending it to the port
	 which has the printer, as in:

		cat myfile >/dev/tty0      - or -
 		pr myfile >/dev/tty0        Try both of these. pr gives
						nice headings.

	 The disadvantage of printing directly is that your terminal is
	 hung up during the time that the report is being printed (which
	 is not the case when spooling.) Also, you or other users  run
         the risk of accidentally sending more than 1 file to the 
         printer at once and making a big mess.
